👉 Tuple is an ordered, immutable collection of elements in Python. It can store multiple values of different data types in a single variable.
  👉 Ordered
  👉 Immutable
  👉 Allows duplicate values
  👉 Can store multiple data types
  👉 Indexed
  👉 Supports slicing
  👉 Faster than List (generally)

t = ()
print(t)   # Empty Tuple

student = ("Rahul", 18, 92.5, True)  # Mixed Data Types
print(student)

data = ((1, 2), (3, 4), (5, 6))  # Nested Tuple
print(data)

t = 10, 20, 30          # Creating Tuple Without Parentheses
print(t)
print(type(t))

numbers = tuple([1, 2, 3, 4])      # Using tuple() Constructor
print(numbers)

  👉 Tuple is an ordered collection.
  👉 Tuple is immutable.
  👉 Duplicate values are allowed.
  👉 Different data types can be stored together.
  👉 Parentheses () are commonly used.
  👉 A single-element tuple must have a trailing comma.
  👉 tuple() constructor can convert other iterables into tuples.

--------------------------------
| Indexing & Negative Indexing |
--------------------------------
👉 Indexing is the process of accessing individual elements of a tuple using their position (index).

  Tuple = ("Apple", "Banana", "Mango", "Orange")
  Index =   0         1         2         3

  fruits = ("Apple", "Banana", "Mango", "Orange")
  print(fruits[0])


  Tuple = ("Apple", "Banana", "Mango", "Orange")          # Negative Indexing
  Positive Index :  0        1         2         3
  Negative Index : -4       -3        -2        -1
